<section id="services" class="wrap">
  <div class="sec-head rv">
    <span class="eyebrow">Google Ads services</span>
    <h2>Every campaign type, <span class="hl">managed in-house</span></h2>
    <p>Google Ads is nine different products sharing one login. Each is bought differently, and most accounts only need three or four of them working well.</p>
  </div>
  <p class="muted rv" style="margin:-6px 0 26px">
        Detail on the work behind each of these:
        <a href="/google-ads-audit/" style="color:var(--purple);font-weight:700">account audits</a>,
        <a href="/google-ads-for-ecommerce/" style="color:var(--purple);font-weight:700">e-commerce</a> and
        <a href="/google-ads-for-local-services/" style="color:var(--purple);font-weight:700">local service businesses</a>.
      </p>
      <div class="channels stg">
    <div class="ch"><div class="ch-top"><h3>Search campaigns</h3><span class="unit">Intent</span></div><p>The highest-intent traffic on the internet. Keyword research, match type structure, ad copy testing and weekly negative keyword work to stop budget leaking into irrelevant queries.</p></div>
    <div class="ch"><div class="ch-top"><h3>Shopping campaigns</h3><span class="unit">E-commerce</span></div><p>Merchant Center setup, feed optimisation and campaign structure split by margin and brand. Bids can't fix a bad product title, so the feed is treated as part of the campaign.</p></div>
    <div class="ch"><div class="ch-top"><h3>Performance Max</h3><span class="unit">Automated</span></div><p>Powerful and opaque. I structure asset groups deliberately, exclude brand traffic where it inflates results, and pull the placement data Google doesn't surface by default.</p></div>
    <div class="ch"><div class="ch-top"><h3>YouTube &amp; video</h3><span class="unit">Video</span></div><p>In-stream, in-feed, Shorts and bumper ads. Hook testing in the first five seconds decides cost per view, so creative iteration is planned alongside media buying.</p></div>
    <div class="ch"><div class="ch-top"><h3>Display campaigns</h3><span class="unit">Reach</span></div><p>Cheap impressions and easy waste. Aggressive placement exclusions, app inventory removal and frequency capping do more for Display performance than targeting changes.</p></div>
    <div class="ch"><div class="ch-top"><h3>Demand Gen</h3><span class="unit">Discovery</span></div><p>Creative-led campaigns across YouTube, Discover and Gmail, using your first-party audiences and lookalike segments to find demand before it reaches search.</p></div>
    <div class="ch"><div class="ch-top"><h3>Remarketing &amp; RLSA</h3><span class="unit">Return</span></div><p>Dynamic remarketing, Customer Match and GA4 audiences, with converters excluded so you stop paying to reach people who already bought.</p></div>
    <div class="ch"><div class="ch-top"><h3>Local Services Ads</h3><span class="unit">Local</span></div><p>LSA verification, Google Business Profile linking, call tracking and geo bid adjustments for businesses that win work by phone rather than by form.</p></div>
    <div class="ch"><div class="ch-top"><h3>Tracking &amp; audits</h3><span class="unit new">Foundation</span></div><p>GA4, Google Tag Manager, enhanced conversions and offline conversion imports. Every engagement starts here, because everything after it depends on the numbers being real.</p></div>
  </div>
  <p class="sec-head rv" style="margin-top:34px;margin-bottom:0"><a href="#campaign-types" class="btn ghost">Full service breakdown <span class="arw">&rarr;</span></a></p>
</section>

<section class="wrap pt0" id="campaign-types">
  <div class="rv">
    <details id="search">
          <summary><h3 class="sum-t"><span>1. Search campaigns</span> <span class="unit">Highest intent</span></h3></summary>
          <div class="plat">
            <p>Someone typing your product into Google is the closest thing to a raised hand you'll get. Search is also where structure matters most: match types, negatives and ad relevance decide whether you pay $2 or $9 for the same click.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Keyword research &amp; grouping</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Match type strategy</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Negative keyword management</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Responsive search ad testing</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Pinning &amp; asset strategy</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Sitelinks, callouts &amp; snippets</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Brand vs non-brand separation</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Quality Score diagnosis</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Auction insights monitoring</li>
      </ul>
          </div>
        </details>

    <details id="shopping">
          <summary><h3 class="sum-t"><span>2. Shopping campaigns</span> <span class="unit">E-commerce</span></h3></summary>
          <div class="plat">
            <p>Shopping performance is decided in the product feed more than in the campaign. Manufacturer titles and missing attributes stop Google understanding what you sell, so feed work comes before bid work.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Merchant Center setup</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Feed diagnostics &amp; error fixes</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Product title rewriting</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Attribute &amp; GTIN completion</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Custom labels by margin</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Campaign priority structure</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Promotions &amp; sale price feeds</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Bestseller segmentation</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Target ROAS management</li>
      </ul>
          </div>
        </details>

    <details id="performance-max">
          <summary><h3 class="sum-t"><span>3. Performance Max</span> <span class="unit">Automated</span></h3></summary>
          <div class="plat">
            <p>Google's most automated campaign type, and the easiest to be misled by. Left unstructured it absorbs brand traffic, reports it as new demand, and looks excellent while cannibalising what you already had.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Asset group structure by product line</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Audience signal configuration</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Brand keyword exclusions</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Search theme setup</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Listing group segmentation</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Placement &amp; channel reporting</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>New customer acquisition goals</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Budget guardrails</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Creative asset refresh cycles</li>
      </ul>
          </div>
        </details>

    <details id="youtube">
          <summary><h3 class="sum-t"><span>4. YouTube &amp; video ads</span> <span class="unit">Video</span></h3></summary>
          <div class="plat">
            <p>The first five seconds decide everything. I plan creative iteration alongside media buying, because on video the hook moves cost per result far more than targeting adjustments do.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Skippable in-stream ads</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>In-feed video ads</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>YouTube Shorts placements</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Bumper ads</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Video ad sequencing</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Custom intent &amp; affinity audiences</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Hook &amp; thumbnail testing</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Viewer remarketing lists</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>View-through conversion analysis</li>
      </ul>
          </div>
        </details>

    <details id="display">
          <summary><h3 class="sum-t"><span>5. Display campaigns</span> <span class="unit">Reach</span></h3></summary>
          <div class="plat">
            <p>Cheap impressions, easy waste. Most underperforming Display campaigns aren't badly targeted. They're running on mobile game inventory nobody excluded. Exclusion lists do the heavy lifting here.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Placement exclusion lists</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Mobile app inventory removal</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Topic &amp; contextual targeting</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Custom segments</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Responsive display assets</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Frequency capping</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Brand safety controls</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Content suitability settings</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Placement performance reviews</li>
      </ul>
          </div>
        </details>

    <details id="demand-gen">
          <summary><h3 class="sum-t"><span>6. Demand Gen</span> <span class="unit">Discovery</span></h3></summary>
          <div class="plat">
            <p>Creative-led campaigns across YouTube, Discover and Gmail, designed to create demand before anyone searches. Works best fed by your own customer data rather than broad interest targeting.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Multi-format asset production briefs</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Customer Match audience upload</li>
        <li><i>&check;</i>Lookalike segment building</li>
