<section class="wrap pt0" id="findings">
  <div class="sec-head rv">
    <span class="eyebrow">The $100 analysis</span>
    <h2>What I look for first in an <span class="hl">existing account</span></h2>
    <p>Six problems turn up often enough that I check for them before anything else. None are exotic, and every one of them costs money for as long as it goes unnoticed.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="channels stg">
    <div class="ch">
      <div class="ch-top"><h3>Conversion tracking that counts twice</h3><span class="unit">Tracking</span></div>
      <p>The same purchase firing from a hard-coded tag and a GA4 import, or a thank-you page counted on every refresh. Smart Bidding then optimises towards a number that was never real, and every report built on top of it inherits the error.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="ch">
      <div class="ch-top"><h3>Branded search eating the budget</h3><span class="unit">Wasted spend</span></div>
      <p>Brand terms convert cheaply and make an account look healthy, but most of those clicks were already yours for free. I separate brand from non-brand before judging anything, because averaging the two hides what the account is actually buying.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="ch">
      <div class="ch-top"><h3>Performance Max with no brand exclusions</h3><span class="unit">PMax</span></div>
      <p>Left unrestricted, PMax absorbs existing demand and reports it as new business. Without brand exclusions and a channel-level breakdown there is no way to tell what it genuinely added.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="ch">
      <div class="ch-top"><h3>Broad match with a thin negative list</h3><span class="unit">Search</span></div>
      <p>Broad match is only as good as the negatives behind it. I pull the search terms report back as far as it goes and usually find whole categories of traffic nobody chose to pay for.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="ch">
      <div class="ch-top"><h3>One conversion action for everything</h3><span class="unit">Bidding</span></div>
      <p>A newsletter signup and a signed contract weighted the same teaches the algorithm to chase whichever is easiest. Conversion values have to reflect what each action is actually worth to the business.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="ch">
      <div class="ch-top"><h3>A product feed doing the campaign's job</h3><span class="unit">Shopping</span></div>
      <p>Manufacturer titles, missing attributes and stale availability decide Shopping performance long before bidding does. Most feed problems are invisible in the Ads interface and only show up in Merchant Center.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

